    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-131c",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "131C Saving of other laws and remedies",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-131c"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.124",
      "values:originalText": "(1) This Part is not intended to exclude or limit the concurrent\noperation of any law, whether written or unwritten, of a State or a\nTerritory.\n\n(2) Section 73 of the Australian Consumer Law does not operate in a\nState or a Territory to the extent necessary to ensure that no\ninconsistency arises between:\n\n(a) that section; and\n\n(b) a provision of a law of the State or Territory that would, but\nfor this subsection, be inconsistent with that section.\n\n(3) Despite subsection (1):\n\n(a) if an act or omission of a person is both:\n\n(i) an offence against this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw; and\n\n(ii) an offence against a law of a State or a Territory; and\n\n(b) the person is convicted of either of those offences;\nhe or she is not liable to be convicted of the other of those\noffences.\n\n(4) Except as expressly provided by this Part or the Australian\nConsumer Law, nothing in this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw is taken to limit, restrict or otherwise affect any right or\nremedy a person would have had if this Part and the Australian\nConsumer Law had not been enacted.\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Division\n\nSection 131D\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nSubdivision B—Effect of other Commonwealth laws on the\nAustralian Consumer Law",
      "skos:definition": "(1) This Part is not intended to exclude or limit the concurrent\noperation of any law, whether written or unwritten, of a State or a\nTerritory.\n\n(2) Section 73 of the Australian Consumer Law does not operate in a\nState or a Territory to the extent necessary to ensure that no\ninconsistency arises between:\n\n(a) that section; and\n\n(b) a provision of a law of the State or Territory that would, but\nfor this subsection, be inconsistent with that section.\n\n(3) Despite subsection (1):\n\n(a) if an act or omission of a person is both:\n\n(i) an offence against this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw; and\n\n(ii) an offence against a law of a State or a Territory; and\n\n(b) the person is convicted of either of those offences;\nhe or she is not liable to be convicted of the other of those\noffences.\n\n(4) Except as expressly provided by this Part or the Australian\nConsumer Law, nothing in this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw is taken to limit, restrict or otherwise affect any right or\nremedy a person would have had if this Part and the Australian\nConsumer Law had not been enacted.\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Division\n\nSection 131D\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nSubdivision B—Effect of other Commonwealth laws on the\nAustralian Consumer Law",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-131c-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-131c-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-131c-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-131c-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1ffc59f45baadcc493b3bda538b9880a9458bbf208fcb521217941a97e4f0bcc"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-131c",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "131C Saving of other laws and remedies",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 124,
      "values:originalText": "(1) This Part is not intended to exclude or limit the concurrent\noperation of any law, whether written or unwritten, of a State or a\nTerritory.\n\n(2) Section 73 of the Australian Consumer Law does not operate in a\nState or a Territory to the extent necessary to ensure that no\ninconsistency arises between:\n\n(a) that section; and\n\n(b) a provision of a law of the State or Territory that would, but\nfor this subsection, be inconsistent with that section.\n\n(3) Despite subsection (1):\n\n(a) if an act or omission of a person is both:\n\n(i) an offence against this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw; and\n\n(ii) an offence against a law of a State or a Territory; and\n\n(b) the person is convicted of either of those offences;\nhe or she is not liable to be convicted of the other of those\noffences.\n\n(4) Except as expressly provided by this Part or the Australian\nConsumer Law, nothing in this Part or the Australian Consumer\nLaw is taken to limit, restrict or otherwise affect any right or\nremedy a person would have had if this Part and the Australian\nConsumer Law had not been enacted.\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Division\n\nSection 131D\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nSubdivision B—Effect of other Commonwealth laws on the\nAustralian Consumer Law"
    },
