      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "134E Effect of failure to comply with an infringement notice",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134e"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.151",
      "values:originalText": "If:\n\n(a) an infringement notice for an alleged contravention of an\ninfringement notice provision is issued to a person; and\n\n(b) the person fails to pay the penalty specified in the\ninfringement notice within the infringement notice\ncompliance period and in accordance with the notice; and\n\n(c) the infringement notice is not withdrawn under section 134G;\nthe person is liable to proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of\nthe Australian Consumer Law in relation to the alleged\ncontravention of the infringement notice provision.",
      "skos:definition": "If:\n\n(a) an infringement notice for an alleged contravention of an\ninfringement notice provision is issued to a person; and\n\n(b) the person fails to pay the penalty specified in the\ninfringement notice within the infringement notice\ncompliance period and in accordance with the notice; and\n\n(c) the infringement notice is not withdrawn under section 134G;\nthe person is liable to proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of\nthe Australian Consumer Law in relation to the alleged\ncontravention of the infringement notice provision.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1c1ecb8afd2e4b4d3b387891d4493e7d7e1c6681931bc8dae43d36f84b5812e6",
      "skos:note": "Failure to comply with infringement notice results in liability for alleged contravention."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134e",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "134E Effect of failure to comply with an infringement notice",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 151,
      "values:originalText": "If:\n\n(a) an infringement notice for an alleged contravention of an\ninfringement notice provision is issued to a person; and\n\n(b) the person fails to pay the penalty specified in the\ninfringement notice within the infringement notice\ncompliance period and in accordance with the notice; and\n\n(c) the infringement notice is not withdrawn under section 134G;\nthe person is liable to proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of\nthe Australian Consumer Law in relation to the alleged\ncontravention of the infringement notice provision."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134e-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "obligation",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Obligation to comply with infringement notice.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134f",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "134F Infringement notice compliance period for infringement notice",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134f"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.151",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this section, the infringement notice compliance period\nfor an infringement notice is the period of 28 days beginning on the\nday after the day on which the infringement notice is issued by the\nCommission.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to this section, the infringement notice compliance period\nfor an infringement notice is the period of 28 days beginning on the\nday after the day on which the infringement notice is issued by the\nCommission.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134f-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "7bef9f9f3a7843326b368bc9ca213474e5f5ce35303329cd560262825e0417e4",
      "skos:note": "Defines infringement notice compliance period"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134f",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "134F Infringement notice compliance period for infringement notice",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 151,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this section, the infringement notice compliance period\nfor an infringement notice is the period of 28 days beginning on the\nday after the day on which the infringement notice is issued by the\nCommission.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134f-norm",
      "@type": "values:Undertaking",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134f"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "134G Withdrawal of an infringement notice",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134g"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.152",
      "values:originalText": "Representations to the Commission\n\n(1) The person to whom an infringement notice has been issued for an\nalleged contravention of an infringement notice provision may\nmake written representations to the Commission seeking the\nwithdrawal of the infringement notice.\n\n(2) Evidence or information that the person, or a representative of the\nperson, gives to the Commission in the course of making\nrepresentations under subsection (1) is not admissible in evidence\nagainst the person or representative in any proceedings (other than\nproceedings for an offence based on the evidence or information\ngiven being false or misleading).\nWithdrawal by the Commission\n\n(3) The Commission may, by written notice (the withdrawal notice)\ngiven to the person to whom an infringement notice was issued,\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nInfringement notices  Division 5\n\nSection 134G\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nwithdraw the infringement notice if the Commission is satisfied\nthat it is appropriate to do so.\n\n(4) Subsection (3) applies whether or not the person has made\nrepresentations seeking the withdrawal.\nContent of withdrawal notices\n\n(5) The withdrawal notice must state:\n\n(a) the name and address of the person; and\n\n(b) the day on which the infringement notice was issued to the\nperson; and\n\n(c) that the infringement notice is withdrawn; and\n\n(d) that proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of the\nAustralian Consumer Law may be started or continued\nagainst the person in relation to:\n\n(i) the alleged contravention of the infringement notice\nprovision; or\n\n(ii) an offence constituted by the same conduct that\nconstituted the alleged contravention.\nTime limit for giving withdrawal notices\n\n(6) To be effective, the withdrawal notice must be given to the person\nwithin the infringement notice compliance period for the\ninfringement notice.\nRefunds\n\n(7) If the infringement notice is withdrawn after the person has paid\nthe penalty specified in the infringement notice, the Commission\nmust refund to the person an amount equal to the amount paid.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "skos:definition": "Representations to the Commission\n\n(1) The person to whom an infringement notice has been issued for an\nalleged contravention of an infringement notice provision may\nmake written representations to the Commission seeking the\nwithdrawal of the infringement notice.\n\n(2) Evidence or information that the person, or a representative of the\nperson, gives to the Commission in the course of making\nrepresentations under subsection (1) is not admissible in evidence\nagainst the person or representative in any proceedings (other than\nproceedings for an offence based on the evidence or information\ngiven being false or misleading).\nWithdrawal by the Commission\n\n(3) The Commission may, by written notice (the withdrawal notice)\ngiven to the person to whom an infringement notice was issued,\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nInfringement notices  Division 5\n\nSection 134G\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nwithdraw the infringement notice if the Commission is satisfied\nthat it is appropriate to do so.\n\n(4) Subsection (3) applies whether or not the person has made\nrepresentations seeking the withdrawal.\nContent of withdrawal notices\n\n(5) The withdrawal notice must state:\n\n(a) the name and address of the person; and\n\n(b) the day on which the infringement notice was issued to the\nperson; and\n\n(c) that the infringement notice is withdrawn; and\n\n(d) that proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of the\nAustralian Consumer Law may be started or continued\nagainst the person in relation to:\n\n(i) the alleged contravention of the infringement notice\nprovision; or\n\n(ii) an offence constituted by the same conduct that\nconstituted the alleged contravention.\nTime limit for giving withdrawal notices\n\n(6) To be effective, the withdrawal notice must be given to the person\nwithin the infringement notice compliance period for the\ninfringement notice.\nRefunds\n\n(7) If the infringement notice is withdra",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-6"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-7"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "ef93c7ed82e299d2a9ddd75bf5a0d6172ef5b4df452146e687a915b2116ef8bb"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134g",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "134G Withdrawal of an infringement notice",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 152,
      "values:originalText": "Representations to the Commission\n\n(1) The person to whom an infringement notice has been issued for an\nalleged contravention of an infringement notice provision may\nmake written representations to the Commission seeking the\nwithdrawal of the infringement notice.\n\n(2) Evidence or information that the person, or a representative of the\nperson, gives to the Commission in the course of making\nrepresentations under subsection (1) is not admissible in evidence\nagainst the person or representative in any proceedings (other than\nproceedings for an offence based on the evidence or information\ngiven being false or misleading).\nWithdrawal by the Commission\n\n(3) The Commission may, by written notice (the withdrawal notice)\ngiven to the person to whom an infringement notice was issued,\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nInfringement notices  Division 5\n\nSection 134G\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nwithdraw the infringement notice if the Commission is satisfied\nthat it is appropriate to do so.\n\n(4) Subsection (3) applies whether or not the person has made\nrepresentations seeking the withdrawal.\nContent of withdrawal notices\n\n(5) The withdrawal notice must state:\n\n(a) the name and address of the person; and\n\n(b) the day on which the infringement notice was issued to the\nperson; and\n\n(c) that the infringement notice is withdrawn; and\n\n(d) that proceedings under Chapter 4 or Part 5-2 of the\nAustralian Consumer Law may be started or continued\nagainst the person in relation to:\n\n(i) the alleged contravention of the infringement notice\nprovision; or\n\n(ii) an offence constituted by the same conduct that\nconstituted the alleged contravention.\nTime limit for giving withdrawal notices\n\n(6) To be effective, the withdrawal notice must be given to the person\nwithin the infringement notice compliance period for the\ninfringement notice.\nRefunds\n\n(7) If the infringement notice is withdrawn after the person has paid\nthe penalty specified in the infringement notice, the Commission\nmust refund to the person an amount equal to the amount paid.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-134g-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "134G(1) Withdrawal of an infringement notice",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-134g-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
