    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "135Z Issue of search warrants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-135z"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.170",
      "values:originalText": "Application for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court for a\nwarrant in relation to premises.\nIssue of warrant\n\n(2) The judge may issue the warrant if:\n\n(a) an affidavit has been given to the judge setting out the\ngrounds on which the issue of the warrant is being sought;\nand\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nSearch, seizure and entry  Division 6\n\nSection 136\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(b) the applicant, or some other person, has given to the judge\nsuch further information (if any) as the judge requires\nconcerning the grounds on which the issue of the warrant is\nbeing sought; and\n\n(c) the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for\nissuing the warrant.\nContent of warrant\n\n(3) The warrant must:\n\n(a) specify the purpose for which the warrant is issued; and\n\n(b) describe the premises to which the warrant relates; and\n\n(c) state that the warrant is issued under this section; and\n\n(d) name one or more inspectors; and\n\n(e) authorise the inspector or inspectors so named:\n\n(i) to enter the premises; and\n\n(ii) to exercise search-related powers in relation to the\npremises; and\n\n(f) state whether the entry is authorised to be made at any time\nof the day or night, or during specified hours of the day or\nnight; and\n\n(g) specify a day (which must not be more than 7 days after the\nday the warrant is issued) on which the warrant ceases to be\nin force.\n136  Search warrants by telephone, fax etc.\nApplication for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court by\ntelephone, fax or other electronic means for a warrant under\nsection 135Z in relation to premises if the inspector believes on\nreasonable grounds that the delay that would occur if an\napplication were made in person would frustrate the effective\nexecution of the warrant.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "skos:definition": "Application for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court for a\nwarrant in relation to premises.\nIssue of warrant\n\n(2) The judge may issue the warrant if:\n\n(a) an affidavit has been given to the judge setting out the\ngrounds on which the issue of the warrant is being sought;\nand\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nSearch, seizure and entry  Division 6\n\nSection 136\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(b) the applicant, or some other person, has given to the judge\nsuch further information (if any) as the judge requires\nconcerning the grounds on which the issue of the warrant is\nbeing sought; and\n\n(c) the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for\nissuing the warrant.\nContent of warrant\n\n(3) The warrant must:\n\n(a) specify the purpose for which the warrant is issued; and\n\n(b) describe the premises to which the warrant relates; and\n\n(c) state that the warrant is issued under this section; and\n\n(d) name one or more inspectors; and\n\n(e) authorise the inspector or inspectors so named:\n\n(i) to enter the premises; and\n\n(ii) to exercise search-related powers in relation to the\npremises; and\n\n(f) state whether the entry is authorised to be made at any time\nof the day or night, or during specified hours of the day or\nnight; and\n\n(g) specify a day (which must not be more than 7 days after the\nday the warrant is issued) on which the warrant ceases to be\nin force.\n136  Search warrants by telephone, fax etc.\nApplication for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court by\ntelephone, fax or other electronic means for a warrant under\nsection 135Z in relation to premises if the inspector believes on\nreasonable grounds that the delay that would occur if an\napplication were made in person would frustrate the effective\nexecution of the warrant.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z-ss-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8642386535e56cb21f292843aef8a764cdcc5e226d757ff570290f1f9732914b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-135z",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "135Z Issue of search warrants",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 170,
      "values:originalText": "Application for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court for a\nwarrant in relation to premises.\nIssue of warrant\n\n(2) The judge may issue the warrant if:\n\n(a) an affidavit has been given to the judge setting out the\ngrounds on which the issue of the warrant is being sought;\nand\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nApplication of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI\nSearch, seizure and entry  Division 6\n\nSection 136\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(b) the applicant, or some other person, has given to the judge\nsuch further information (if any) as the judge requires\nconcerning the grounds on which the issue of the warrant is\nbeing sought; and\n\n(c) the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for\nissuing the warrant.\nContent of warrant\n\n(3) The warrant must:\n\n(a) specify the purpose for which the warrant is issued; and\n\n(b) describe the premises to which the warrant relates; and\n\n(c) state that the warrant is issued under this section; and\n\n(d) name one or more inspectors; and\n\n(e) authorise the inspector or inspectors so named:\n\n(i) to enter the premises; and\n\n(ii) to exercise search-related powers in relation to the\npremises; and\n\n(f) state whether the entry is authorised to be made at any time\nof the day or night, or during specified hours of the day or\nnight; and\n\n(g) specify a day (which must not be more than 7 days after the\nday the warrant is issued) on which the warrant ceases to be\nin force.\n136  Search warrants by telephone, fax etc.\nApplication for warrant\n\n(1) An inspector may apply to a judge of the Federal Court by\ntelephone, fax or other electronic means for a warrant under\nsection 135Z in relation to premises if the inspector believes on\nreasonable grounds that the delay that would occur if an\napplication were made in person would frustrate the effective\nexecution of the warrant.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135z-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "135Z(1) Issue of search warrants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-135z-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
