    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "138A Conferring jurisdiction on the Federal Circuit and Family",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-138a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.185",
      "values:originalText": "Court of Australia (Division 2)\n\n(1) Subject to this section, jurisdiction is conferred on the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) in relation to\nany matter arising under this Part or the Australian Consumer Law\nin respect of which a civil proceeding is instituted by a person\nother than the Commonwealth Minister.\n\n(2) If proceedings under Part 3-5, or section 236, of the Australian\nConsumer Law are instituted in, or transferred to, the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court does\nnot have jurisdiction to award an amount for loss or damage that\nexceeds:\n\n(a) $750,000; or\n\n(b) if another amount is specified in the regulations—that other\namount.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "skos:definition": "Court of Australia (Division 2)\n\n(1) Subject to this section, jurisdiction is conferred on the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) in relation to\nany matter arising under this Part or the Australian Consumer Law\nin respect of which a civil proceeding is instituted by a person\nother than the Commonwealth Minister.\n\n(2) If proceedings under Part 3-5, or section 236, of the Australian\nConsumer Law are instituted in, or transferred to, the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court does\nnot have jurisdiction to award an amount for loss or damage that\nexceeds:\n\n(a) $750,000; or\n\n(b) if another amount is specified in the regulations—that other\namount.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138a-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4f73cc7aeefa1bef3807fef1e13680564c6f3934be92ce11079c60eb48a46434"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-138a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "138A Conferring jurisdiction on the Federal Circuit and Family",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 185,
      "values:originalText": "Court of Australia (Division 2)\n\n(1) Subject to this section, jurisdiction is conferred on the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) in relation to\nany matter arising under this Part or the Australian Consumer Law\nin respect of which a civil proceeding is instituted by a person\nother than the Commonwealth Minister.\n\n(2) If proceedings under Part 3-5, or section 236, of the Australian\nConsumer Law are instituted in, or transferred to, the Federal\nCircuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), the Court does\nnot have jurisdiction to award an amount for loss or damage that\nexceeds:\n\n(a) $750,000; or\n\n(b) if another amount is specified in the regulations—that other\namount.\n\nChapter 6  Other provisions"
    },
