    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138b",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "138B Conferring jurisdiction on State and Territory Courts",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-138b"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.186",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Jurisdiction is conferred on the several courts of the States and\nTerritories in relation to any matter arising under this Part or the\nAustralian Consumer Law in respect of which a civil proceeding is\ninstituted by a person other than the Commonwealth Minister or\nthe Commission.\n\n(3) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the States is conferred within the limits of their several\njurisdictions, whether those limits are as to locality, subject matter\nor otherwise.\n\n(4) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the Territories is conferred to the extent that the Constitution\npermits.\n\n(5) This section is not to be taken to enable an inferior court of a State\nor a Territory to grant a remedy other than a remedy of a kind that\nthe court is able to grant under the law of that State or Territory.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Jurisdiction is conferred on the several courts of the States and\nTerritories in relation to any matter arising under this Part or the\nAustralian Consumer Law in respect of which a civil proceeding is\ninstituted by a person other than the Commonwealth Minister or\nthe Commission.\n\n(3) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the States is conferred within the limits of their several\njurisdictions, whether those limits are as to locality, subject matter\nor otherwise.\n\n(4) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the Territories is conferred to the extent that the Constitution\npermits.\n\n(5) This section is not to be taken to enable an inferior court of a State\nor a Territory to grant a remedy other than a remedy of a kind that\nthe court is able to grant under the law of that State or Territory.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138b-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138b-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138b-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138b-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b1f3dee482062816fdbe83a2fdb728681c2bb2fde97d32941bb864dc345afd0a"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-138b",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "138B Conferring jurisdiction on State and Territory Courts",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 186,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Jurisdiction is conferred on the several courts of the States and\nTerritories in relation to any matter arising under this Part or the\nAustralian Consumer Law in respect of which a civil proceeding is\ninstituted by a person other than the Commonwealth Minister or\nthe Commission.\n\n(3) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the States is conferred within the limits of their several\njurisdictions, whether those limits are as to locality, subject matter\nor otherwise.\n\n(4) The jurisdiction conferred by subsection (1) on the several courts\nof the Territories is conferred to the extent that the Constitution\npermits.\n\n(5) This section is not to be taken to enable an inferior court of a State\nor a Territory to grant a remedy other than a remedy of a kind that\nthe court is able to grant under the law of that State or Territory."
    },
