      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-150f",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "150F Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-150f"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.208",
      "values:originalText": "Commonwealth entities\n\n(1) An application law may confer functions or powers, or impose\nduties, on a Commonwealth entity for the purposes of the\nCompetition Code.\nNote:\nSection 150FB sets out when such a law imposes a duty on a\nCommonwealth entity.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not authorise the conferral of a function or\npower, or the imposition of a duty, by an application law to the\nextent to which:\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nThe Competition Code  Part XIA\n\nSection 150FA\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the conferral or imposition, or the authorisation, would\ncontravene any constitutional doctrines restricting the duties\nthat may be imposed on the Commonwealth entity; or\n\n(b) the authorisation would otherwise exceed the legislative\npower of the Commonwealth.\n\n(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or\nexercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of\nthe function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in\naccordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the\nState or Territory concerned.",
      "skos:definition": "Commonwealth entities\n\n(1) An application law may confer functions or powers, or impose\nduties, on a Commonwealth entity for the purposes of the\nCompetition Code.\nNote:\nSection 150FB sets out when such a law imposes a duty on a\nCommonwealth entity.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not authorise the conferral of a function or\npower, or the imposition of a duty, by an application law to the\nextent to which:\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nThe Competition Code  Part XIA\n\nSection 150FA\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the conferral or imposition, or the authorisation, would\ncontravene any constitutional doctrines restricting the duties\nthat may be imposed on the Commonwealth entity; or\n\n(b) the authorisation would otherwise exceed the legislative\npower of the Commonwealth.\n\n(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or\nexercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of\nthe function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in\naccordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the\nState or Territory concerned.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-150f-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-150f-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-150f-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "71049962d275fbcd26928573093aabdd09f9b9fe0ec428f04791964d312fa3c7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-150f",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "150F Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 208,
      "values:originalText": "Commonwealth entities\n\n(1) An application law may confer functions or powers, or impose\nduties, on a Commonwealth entity for the purposes of the\nCompetition Code.\nNote:\nSection 150FB sets out when such a law imposes a duty on a\nCommonwealth entity.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not authorise the conferral of a function or\npower, or the imposition of a duty, by an application law to the\nextent to which:\n\nOther provisions  Chapter 6\nThe Competition Code  Part XIA\n\nSection 150FA\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the conferral or imposition, or the authorisation, would\ncontravene any constitutional doctrines restricting the duties\nthat may be imposed on the Commonwealth entity; or\n\n(b) the authorisation would otherwise exceed the legislative\npower of the Commonwealth.\n\n(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or\nexercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of\nthe function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in\naccordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the\nState or Territory concerned."
    },
