    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-152ac",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "152AC Definitions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-152ac"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.298",
      "values:originalText": "In this Part, unless the contrary intention appears:\naccess has the meaning given by section 152AF.\naccess agreement has the meaning given by section 152BE.\naccess determination means a determination under section 152BC.\naccess seeker has the meaning given by section 152AG.\naccess undertaking means a special access undertaking.\nbinding rules of conduct means rules made under\nsubsection 152BD(1).\ncarriage service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed carriage\nservice.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nTelecommunications access regime  Part XIC\nIntroduction  Division 1\n\nSection 152AC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\ncarriage service provider has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncarrier has the same meaning as in the Telecommunications Act\n1997.\ncarrier licence has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncategory A standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AR.\ncategory B standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AXB.\nconditional-access customer equipment means customer\nequipment that:\n\n(a) consists of or incorporates a conditional access system that\nallows a service provider to determine whether an end-user is\nable to receive a particular service; and\n\n(b) either:\n\n(i) is for use in connection with the supply of a content\nservice; or\n\n(ii) is of a kind specified in the regulations.\nconstitutional corporation means a corporation to which\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution applies.\ncontent service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed content\nservice.\ncustomer equipment has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ndata processing device means any article or material (for example,\na disk) from which information is capable of being reproduced,\nwith or without the aid of any other article or device.\ndeclared service has the meaning given by section 152AL.\n\nChapter 7  Further provisions relating to particular industries etc.",
      "skos:definition": "In this Part, unless the contrary intention appears:\naccess has the meaning given by section 152AF.\naccess agreement has the meaning given by section 152BE.\naccess determination means a determination under section 152BC.\naccess seeker has the meaning given by section 152AG.\naccess undertaking means a special access undertaking.\nbinding rules of conduct means rules made under\nsubsection 152BD(1).\ncarriage service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed carriage\nservice.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nTelecommunications access regime  Part XIC\nIntroduction  Division 1\n\nSection 152AC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\ncarriage service provider has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncarrier has the same meaning as in the Telecommunications Act\n1997.\ncarrier licence has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncategory A standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AR.\ncategory B standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AXB.\nconditional-access customer equipment means customer\nequipment that:\n\n(a) consists of or incorporates a conditional access system that\nallows a service provider to determine whether an end-user is\nable to receive a particular service; and\n\n(b) either:\n\n(i) is for use in connection with the supply of a content\nservice; or\n\n(ii) is of a kind specified in the regulations.\nconstitutional corporation means a corporation to which\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution applies.\ncontent service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed content\nservice.\ncustomer equipment has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ndata processing device means any article or material (for example,\na disk) from which information is capable of being reproduced,\nwith or without the aid of any other article or device.\ndeclared service has",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-152ac-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-152ac-logic-descriptionlogic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-152ac-logic-descriptionlogic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "a3d51ea94c0f9d848ee1f9eccf5b58d644da6a5cd0b741df7ad72fc27bfac11e",
      "skos:note": "Definitions for Telecommunications access regime"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-152ac",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "152AC Definitions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 298,
      "values:originalText": "In this Part, unless the contrary intention appears:\naccess has the meaning given by section 152AF.\naccess agreement has the meaning given by section 152BE.\naccess determination means a determination under section 152BC.\naccess seeker has the meaning given by section 152AG.\naccess undertaking means a special access undertaking.\nbinding rules of conduct means rules made under\nsubsection 152BD(1).\ncarriage service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed carriage\nservice.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nTelecommunications access regime  Part XIC\nIntroduction  Division 1\n\nSection 152AC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\ncarriage service provider has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncarrier has the same meaning as in the Telecommunications Act\n1997.\ncarrier licence has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ncategory A standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AR.\ncategory B standard access obligation has the meaning given by\nsection 152AXB.\nconditional-access customer equipment means customer\nequipment that:\n\n(a) consists of or incorporates a conditional access system that\nallows a service provider to determine whether an end-user is\nable to receive a particular service; and\n\n(b) either:\n\n(i) is for use in connection with the supply of a content\nservice; or\n\n(ii) is of a kind specified in the regulations.\nconstitutional corporation means a corporation to which\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution applies.\ncontent service has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997, and includes a proposed content\nservice.\ncustomer equipment has the same meaning as in the\nTelecommunications Act 1997.\ndata processing device means any article or material (for example,\na disk) from which information is capable of being reproduced,\nwith or without the aid of any other article or device.\ndeclared service has the meaning given by section 152AL.\n\nChapter 7  Further provisions relating to particular industries etc."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-152ac-norm",
