      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-153l",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "153L Commission may give draft public warning notice",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-153l"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.438",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Commission may give a corporation a notice in writing if the\nCommission reasonably believes that:\n\n(a) any of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n(i) the corporation has engaged in prohibited conduct;\n\n(ii) the corporation is engaging in prohibited conduct; and\n\n(b) one or more persons has suffered, or is likely to suffer,\ndetriment as a result of the prohibited conduct; and\n\n(c) it is in the public interest to issue the notice.\n\n(2) The notice must:\n\n(a) state the day on which the notice is given; and\n\n(b) identify:\n\n(i) the corporation mentioned in paragraph (1)(a); and\n\n(ii) the prohibited conduct mentioned in paragraph (1)(a);\nand\n\n(c) explain the reasons why the Commission reasonably believes\nthat the requirements in paragraphs (1)(a), (b) and (c) are\nmet; and\n\n(d) state that:\n\n(i) the corporation may, within 21 days after being given\nthe notice, make representations to the Commission\nregarding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (1)(a),\n(b) and (c); and\n\n(ii) the Commission may issue a public warning notice\nunder section 153M in relation to the prohibited conduct\nafter those 21 days have passed.\n\n(3) A notice given under subsection (1) is not a legislative instrument.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nThe Electricity Industry  Part XICA\nCommission responses  Division 3\n\nSection 153M\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Commission may give a corporation a notice in writing if the\nCommission reasonably believes that:\n\n(a) any of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n(i) the corporation has engaged in prohibited conduct;\n\n(ii) the corporation is engaging in prohibited conduct; and\n\n(b) one or more persons has suffered, or is likely to suffer,\ndetriment as a result of the prohibited conduct; and\n\n(c) it is in the public interest to issue the notice.\n\n(2) The notice must:\n\n(a) state the day on which the notice is given; and\n\n(b) identify:\n\n(i) the corporation mentioned in paragraph (1)(a); and\n\n(ii) the prohibited conduct mentioned in paragraph (1)(a);\nand\n\n(c) explain the reasons why the Commission reasonably believes\nthat the requirements in paragraphs (1)(a), (b) and (c) are\nmet; and\n\n(d) state that:\n\n(i) the corporation may, within 21 days after being given\nthe notice, make representations to the Commission\nregarding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (1)(a),\n(b) and (c); and\n\n(ii) the Commission may issue a public warning notice\nunder section 153M in relation to the prohibited conduct\nafter those 21 days have passed.\n\n(3) A notice given under subsection (1) is not a legislative instrument.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nThe Electricity Industry  Part XICA\nCommission responses  Division 3\n\nSection 153M\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-153l-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-153l-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-153l-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "65d9ccc48b88dfad1aa21d9b0143b824ccf7ba70d0423ca0cab019d42d96d512"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-153l",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "153L Commission may give draft public warning notice",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 438,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Commission may give a corporation a notice in writing if the\nCommission reasonably believes that:\n\n(a) any of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n(i) the corporation has engaged in prohibited conduct;\n\n(ii) the corporation is engaging in prohibited conduct; and\n\n(b) one or more persons has suffered, or is likely to suffer,\ndetriment as a result of the prohibited conduct; and\n\n(c) it is in the public interest to issue the notice.\n\n(2) The notice must:\n\n(a) state the day on which the notice is given; and\n\n(b) identify:\n\n(i) the corporation mentioned in paragraph (1)(a); and\n\n(ii) the prohibited conduct mentioned in paragraph (1)(a);\nand\n\n(c) explain the reasons why the Commission reasonably believes\nthat the requirements in paragraphs (1)(a), (b) and (c) are\nmet; and\n\n(d) state that:\n\n(i) the corporation may, within 21 days after being given\nthe notice, make representations to the Commission\nregarding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (1)(a),\n(b) and (c); and\n\n(ii) the Commission may issue a public warning notice\nunder section 153M in relation to the prohibited conduct\nafter those 21 days have passed.\n\n(3) A notice given under subsection (1) is not a legislative instrument.\n\nFurther provisions relating to particular industries etc.  Chapter 7\nThe Electricity Industry  Part XICA\nCommission responses  Division 3\n\nSection 153M\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026"
    },
