    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-154u",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "154U Return of seized things",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-154u"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.508",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if a person (the seizer)\nseizes a thing under this Division, the person must return it if:\n\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth or is the subject of a dispute as to ownership.\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nSearch and seizure  Part XID\nEntry to premises under a search warrant  Division 4\n\nSection 154V\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), the seizer\nmust take reasonable steps to return the thing to the person from\nwhom it was seized (or to the owner if that person is not entitled to\npossess it), unless:\n\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n\n(b) an inspector may retain the thing because of an order under\nsection 154V; or\n\n(c) the seizer is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an order of a\ncourt, of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory) to\nretain, destroy or dispose of the thing.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if a person (the seizer)\nseizes a thing under this Division, the person must return it if:\n\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth or is the subject of a dispute as to ownership.\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nSearch and seizure  Part XID\nEntry to premises under a search warrant  Division 4\n\nSection 154V\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), the seizer\nmust take reasonable steps to return the thing to the person from\nwhom it was seized (or to the owner if that person is not entitled to\npossess it), unless:\n\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n\n(b) an inspector may retain the thing because of an order under\nsection 154V; or\n\n(c) the seizer is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an order of a\ncourt, of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory) to\nretain, destroy or dispose of the thing.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-154u-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-154u-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "49a84a52b08508b573b2fb5d7e14abe2e54ea27272624d750c1425251e52cb7d"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-154u",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "154U Return of seized things",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 508,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if a person (the seizer)\nseizes a thing under this Division, the person must return it if:\n\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth or is the subject of a dispute as to ownership.\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nSearch and seizure  Part XID\nEntry to premises under a search warrant  Division 4\n\nSection 154V\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), the seizer\nmust take reasonable steps to return the thing to the person from\nwhom it was seized (or to the owner if that person is not entitled to\npossess it), unless:\n\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n\n(b) an inspector may retain the thing because of an order under\nsection 154V; or\n\n(c) the seizer is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an order of a\ncourt, of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory) to\nretain, destroy or dispose of the thing."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-154u-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
