    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-157d",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "157D General powers of a court",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-157d"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.564",
      "values:originalText": "Power of a court in a criminal or civil proceeding\n\n(1) The power of a court to control the conduct of a criminal or civil\nproceeding, in particular with respect to abuse of process, is not\naffected by section 157B or 157C, except so far as that section\nexpressly or impliedly provides otherwise.\nStay order—criminal proceeding\n\n(2) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a criminal proceeding\nbe stayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on a defendant’s right to receive a fair hearing.\nStay order—civil proceeding\n\n(3) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a civil proceeding be\nstayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on the hearing in the proceeding.\n\n(4) In deciding whether to order a stay of the civil proceeding, the\ncourt must consider:\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nMiscellaneous  Part XII\n\nSection 158\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the extent of any financial loss that a party would suffer as a\nresult of the proceeding being stayed; and\n\n(b) whether a party has reasonable prospects of obtaining a\nremedy in the proceeding; and\n\n(c) any other matter the court considers relevant.",
      "skos:definition": "Power of a court in a criminal or civil proceeding\n\n(1) The power of a court to control the conduct of a criminal or civil\nproceeding, in particular with respect to abuse of process, is not\naffected by section 157B or 157C, except so far as that section\nexpressly or impliedly provides otherwise.\nStay order—criminal proceeding\n\n(2) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a criminal proceeding\nbe stayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on a defendant’s right to receive a fair hearing.\nStay order—civil proceeding\n\n(3) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a civil proceeding be\nstayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on the hearing in the proceeding.\n\n(4) In deciding whether to order a stay of the civil proceeding, the\ncourt must consider:\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nMiscellaneous  Part XII\n\nSection 158\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the extent of any financial loss that a party would suffer as a\nresult of the proceeding being stayed; and\n\n(b) whether a party has reasonable prospects of obtaining a\nremedy in the proceeding; and\n\n(c) any other matter the court considers relevant.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-157d-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-157d-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-157d-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-157d-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "2ce952a32ebdaa3d6b985dec30c3d2dc148de43354f1d60625189a520aa5d6f2"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-157d",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "157D General powers of a court",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 564,
      "values:originalText": "Power of a court in a criminal or civil proceeding\n\n(1) The power of a court to control the conduct of a criminal or civil\nproceeding, in particular with respect to abuse of process, is not\naffected by section 157B or 157C, except so far as that section\nexpressly or impliedly provides otherwise.\nStay order—criminal proceeding\n\n(2) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a criminal proceeding\nbe stayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on a defendant’s right to receive a fair hearing.\nStay order—civil proceeding\n\n(3) A refusal by a court to grant leave under subsection 157B(1) does\nnot prevent the court from later ordering that a civil proceeding be\nstayed on the ground that the refusal would have a substantial\nadverse effect on the hearing in the proceeding.\n\n(4) In deciding whether to order a stay of the civil proceeding, the\ncourt must consider:\n\nMiscellaneous  Chapter 8\nMiscellaneous  Part XII\n\nSection 158\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(a) the extent of any financial loss that a party would suffer as a\nresult of the proceeding being stayed; and\n\n(b) whether a party has reasonable prospects of obtaining a\nremedy in the proceeding; and\n\n(c) any other matter the court considers relevant."
    },
