    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-160",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "160 Failure of witness to attend",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-160"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.569",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A person served, as prescribed, with a summons to appear as a\nwitness before the Tribunal shall not:\n\n(a) fail to attend as required by the summons; or\n\n(b) fail to appear and report himself or herself from day to day\nunless excused, or released from further attendance, by a\nmember of the Tribunal.\n\n(1A) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsection (1A), see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) is guilty of an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 20 penalty units\nor imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote 1:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\nNote 2:\nPart IA of the Crimes Act 1914 contains provisions dealing with\npenalties.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A person served, as prescribed, with a summons to appear as a\nwitness before the Tribunal shall not:\n\n(a) fail to attend as required by the summons; or\n\n(b) fail to appear and report himself or herself from day to day\nunless excused, or released from further attendance, by a\nmember of the Tribunal.\n\n(1A) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsection (1A), see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) is guilty of an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 20 penalty units\nor imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote 1:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\nNote 2:\nPart IA of the Crimes Act 1914 contains provisions dealing with\npenalties.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-160-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-160-ss-1a"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-160-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "e00bf8b913e184aef3115eb6ec13c82eb3d69a9e3100892143b38f02306b2d16"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-160",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "160 Failure of witness to attend",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 569,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A person served, as prescribed, with a summons to appear as a\nwitness before the Tribunal shall not:\n\n(a) fail to attend as required by the summons; or\n\n(b) fail to appear and report himself or herself from day to day\nunless excused, or released from further attendance, by a\nmember of the Tribunal.\n\n(1A) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsection (1A), see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) is guilty of an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 20 penalty units\nor imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote 1:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\nNote 2:\nPart IA of the Crimes Act 1914 contains provisions dealing with\npenalties."
    },
    {
